// HTML: markup with a path in every `<%= %>`, and one way past escaping.
//
// A page is written as two things that do not know about each other — a
// function that answers an ordinary struct, and a string of markup whose holes
// are paths into it. `fill` puts them together, and **the type of what a path
// reaches decides whether it is escaped**: an `Html` goes in whole because it
// is already markup, and everything else is rendered and escaped. So the
// escaping decision is written once, where the data is declared, rather than
// at each of the places the data is used.
// [ADR 0099](../../../docs/adr/0099-a-page-is-markup-with-a-path-in-every-erb-tag.md)
// is the record of that and of what it was measured against.
//
// ```cove
// use std.html
// use std.html.Html
//
// struct Row {
//   at: String
//   said: String
//   who: Html      // this one is markup, and the declaration says so
// }
//
// fn row(line: Row) -> Html {
//   html.fill(
//     "<tr><td class=at><%= at %></td><%= who %><td><%= said %></td></tr>",
//     line,
//   )
// }
// ```
//
// # Why `<%= %>` and not `{{ }}`
//
// `{{ }}` is what nearly every template language uses — Mustache, Handlebars,
// Jinja, Liquid, Angular, Vue, Go — and Cove cannot have it: interpolation owns
// `{`, so a literal brace in a Cove string is `\{`, and a template full of
// `\{\{ name \}\}` would be unreadable at the one place it has to be read.
// `<%= %>` is the next most recognised pair — ERB, EJS, JSP, ASP — it needs no
// escaping in a Cove string, and `<%` is not valid HTML, so it collides with no
// markup, no URL and no prose, which `$`, `?`, `[[` and `$(` each could.
//
// The deciding reason is that it brings the right *meaning* with it and not
// only the right shape. **Rails' `<%= %>` already decides escaping by type**:
// an `ActiveSupport::SafeBuffer` goes in whole and a plain `String` is
// escaped, with `raw` and `html_safe` as the single opt-out — and the standard
// warning in that community is that `html_safe` scattered through a view is the
// XSS anti-pattern. That is this module's design exactly: an `Html` goes in
// whole, everything else is escaped, and `markup` is the one name a review
// greps for. A reader who knows Rails already knows what `<%= %>` means here.
//
// Two ways this is stricter than Rails. `Html` is `opaque`, so unlike a
// `SafeBuffer` it cannot be conjured out of any `String` by a method call —
// `fill` and `markup` are the only two things that make one. And `markup` has
// to be imported by name, so the opt-out is in the import list as well as at
// its call site.
//
// What this module is not: a template language. There is no `if` and no `for`
// in a template, because the language already has both, and a second syntax to
// parse, check, format and colour buys nothing that a `for` around a `fill`
// does not. `<% %>` without the `=` — ERB's statement form — has no meaning
// here for the same reason, and is text. There is no element tree either:
// `<td class=at>` is written as `<td class=at>`, not translated into a call.
// And nothing here serves a page — `render` hands back the text, and what is
// done with it belongs to whatever is serving.
//
// # Where a bad hole is answered
//
// A template takes a check away. `{h(app.nmae)}` was a compile error, because
// the page named the field in Cove; `<%= nmae %>` is a string, and nothing
// above the run can read it. Something has to put that check back, and this
// module puts it in `render`, which answers a `Result`.
//
// `fill` does not, and the asymmetry is the point. `fill` runs once per row
// and once per cell, so making it fallible would put a `?` at every one of
// those sites, in code whose whole value is that it reads like the markup it
// makes. `render` runs once per page, at the boundary where markup becomes a
// response — a place that is already fallible. So a bad hole is written down
// on the `Html`, travels with it through every composition, and is answered
// once.
//
// What is refused is a path that does not reach a value — one rule, with no
// exception for an index past the end. A struct's fields are settled when it
// is declared and a fixed index is a claim about the shape of the data, so
// either way a path that misses is a mistake in the template.

use std.stringbuilder.StringBuilder

/// A piece of markup: text that `fill` will put in whole rather than escape.
///
/// It is `opaque`, which is the whole of its safety. The only values of this
/// type are ones [`fill`] built and ones [`markup`] was told about, so
/// `markup` is the single way text can reach a page unescaped — one name for a
/// review to grep for, where an escaping helper called at 179 sites is 179
/// places a reader has to decide about.
///
/// It carries a second thing, and that is what lets [`render`] refuse. A hole
/// whose path names no field is a mistake in the template and there is no
/// input that makes it one, so somebody has to be told. `fill` cannot be the
/// one to tell them — it runs once per row and once per cell — so it writes
/// the problem down here and `render`, which runs once per page, answers it.
export opaque struct Html {
  /// The markup, as it will be written.
  said: String

  /// The first thing that went wrong building it, or `""` where nothing did.
  ///
  /// The *first* and not all of them: a page with two bad holes is one bug
  /// report, and the hole to name is the one a reader will find first in the
  /// template.
  ///
  /// It survives composition, which is the property the whole arrangement
  /// rests on. An `Html` put into another template through a hole hands its
  /// problem to the `Html` that comes out, and so does one that arrives in a
  /// list of markup, so a mistake in the innermost fragment is still there to
  /// be answered at the outermost `render`.
  wrong: String
}

/// Markup that was written by hand, taken at its word.
///
/// **This is the one way past escaping**, so what it is given has to be markup
/// already — a constant in the program, or something another `fill` built.
/// Text that came from outside the program goes through [`fill`] as data,
/// where it is escaped because its type is not this one.
export fn markup(said: String) -> Html {
  Html(said: said, wrong: "")
}

/// No markup at all: what a page puts where a part is absent.
///
/// A field of type `Html` has to hold something, and `""` is not an `Html`, so
/// this is what a `None` becomes on the way into a view model.
export fn empty() -> Html {
  Html(said: "", wrong: "")
}

/// The text of a page, or the first thing that went wrong building it.
///
/// The one way out, as [`markup`] is the one way in. Everything between the
/// two is `Html`, so nothing in the middle of building a page is a `String`
/// that somebody might concatenate unescaped.
///
/// **This is where a bad hole is answered, and it is the only place it could
/// be.** What it refuses is a template that asks for something its data
/// cannot have — `<%= nmae %>` against a struct with a `name`. That is a
/// programming error, no request can cause it, and before this module existed
/// the compiler caught it, because the page named the field in Cove. A
/// template takes that check away, so something has to put it back, and the
/// only honest place is a failing call: a test goes red and a request answers
/// 500, instead of a page shipping with a marker in it.
///
/// The rule is one sentence — a path that does not reach a value is an error
/// — and [`fill`] says why an index past the end is not the exception it
/// looks like.
///
/// **Fallible here and not in `fill`** is the whole of the trade. `fill` runs
/// once per row and once per cell, so a `Result` there would put a `?` or a
/// `match` into code whose point is that it reads like the markup it makes.
/// `render` runs once per page: one `?` at the boundary where markup becomes
/// a response, which is a place that is already fallible for half a dozen
/// other reasons.
export fn render(page: Html) -> Result<String, Error> {
  if page.wrong != "" {
    return Err(Error(page.wrong))
  }
  Ok(page.said)
}

/// `template` with every hole filled from `data`.
///
/// **A hole is `<%=`, a path, and `%>`.** Everything outside a pair of those
/// is text, whatever it holds: `$5.00`, `?over=hour`, `[[x]]`, `$(y)` and
/// `100%` come through as they were written, and so does a `{` — which no
/// sigil-shaped notation could promise, and which is most of why these are the
/// delimiters (see this module's header).
///
/// | written | what it reaches |
/// | --- | --- |
/// | `<%= . %>` | the data itself, as `.` is jq's input |
/// | `<%= name %>` | the field called `name` |
/// | `<%= limits.maxQueued %>` | a field of a field |
/// | `<%= items[2] %>` | the element at index 2 |
///
/// A segment's name is letters, digits and `_`, and an index is `[` digits
/// `]`. **The space inside the delimiters is trimmed**, so `<%=name%>`,
/// `<%= name %>` and `<%=  name  %>` are one hole written three ways — a
/// template is laid out by whoever is reading the markup, not by a scanner.
///
/// **What goes in is decided by the type the path reaches**, never by the
/// template:
///
/// | reached | what goes in |
/// | --- | --- |
/// | an `Html` | its text, whole and unescaped — it is already markup |
/// | an `Array<Html>` or a `Vector<Html>` | each of them, in order, unescaped |
/// | anything else | its text, with `&`, `<`, `>`, `"` and `'` escaped |
///
/// The escaping is the same inside an attribute value as between tags, which
/// is why `"` and `'` are in that list: `<a title="<%= said %>">` holds
/// whatever `said` is, and nothing it can hold closes the quote.
///
/// A value that is not a `String` needs no stringifying by the caller — an
/// `Int`, a `Bool`, a `Float` and a `Duration` are written as the language
/// writes them, and so is a struct or an array of anything that is not markup.
/// An empty `Array` or `Vector` puts in nothing rather than `[]`, because the
/// list a page loops over is usually a list of rows and no rows should be no
/// markup.
///
/// # A path that does not reach a value is an error
///
/// That is the whole of the rule, and it is one sentence on purpose. A
/// segment that names no field — `<%= nmae %>` against a struct with a
/// `name`, or `<%= who.said %>` reaching into an opaque value — is a mistake
/// in the template, because a struct's fields are fixed when it is declared.
/// So is text between the delimiters that is not a path at all: a trailing
/// `.`, a bracket with no number in it, a space in the middle of a name. So
/// is a `<%=` that is never closed. Each is written down on the `Html` and
/// [`render`] refuses with it.
///
/// **An index past the end is not the exception it looks like.** It is
/// tempting to let `<%= items[5] %>` against three items put in nothing, on
/// the grounds that the length depends on the data and a short list is
/// something a page legitimately shows. But a fixed index is the template
/// *asserting* there is a sixth item — a claim about the shape of the data,
/// and a claim that is false is a mistake like any other. A page whose length
/// really does vary loops in Cove and hands over an `Array<Html>`, which is
/// what a list hole and `<%= . %>` are for. (An empty list put in whole still
/// puts in nothing; no rows is no markup. It is indexing that makes a claim.)
///
/// Letting it through would also have been the very shape this module stopped
/// doing: renders something, fails nothing. A hole that quietly contributes
/// nothing is a hole nobody notices, which is the marker's problem wearing
/// different clothes.
///
/// **The delimiters are what make this possible, and that is the second thing
/// they buy.** A hole has a beginning and an end, so the text between them is
/// a path or it is nothing, and a malformed path is refused like a path that
/// misses. Under a sigil the scanner could only read as far as it understood
/// and leave the rest as text, so `$.limits.maxQued` and `$.limits.max Qued`
/// failed in two different ways, one loudly and one not at all.
///
/// # Why a hole that goes wrong puts in nothing
///
/// It used to put in `[no such path: nmae]`, a marker loud enough that a
/// glance at the page would catch it. The marker is gone, and what replaced
/// it is not a quieter version of the same thing but a louder one in a
/// different place.
///
/// A marker is loud in the output and silent everywhere a program is checked.
/// That was measured and not supposed: one hole misspelt on a page that
/// renders on every view of it, and the program's `check` reported nothing
/// and its 58 tests all passed. A page would have shipped reading
/// `[no such path: nmae]`, having passed every gate it had. Loud where a
/// person might look and silent where a machine does look is exactly the
/// wrong way round.
///
/// So the problem is carried instead, and `render` refuses. Once it does, the
/// text of a broken `Html` cannot be observed at all — `render` is the only
/// way out of this type, and a broken `Html` composed into a whole page
/// breaks that page too — so a marker in it would be text nobody can read.
/// Nothing is put in, and the problem goes to the one place that can answer
/// it.
export fn fill(template: String, data: Any) -> Html {
  let end = template.byteLength()
  var out = StringBuilder.withCapacity(end + 32)
  let root = core.dynamicOpen(data)
  var wrong = ""
  var at = 0
  var copied = 0
  while at < end {
    if opensHole(template, at, end) {
      out.appendSlice(template, copied, at)
      let inner = at + opener().byteLength()
      let close = core.stringFind(template, closer(), inner)
      if close < 0 {
        if wrong == "" {
          wrong = unclosed(template)
        }
        at = end
      } else {
        let path = core.stringSlice(template, inner, close - inner)
        let hole = place(root, path.trim(), template)
        out.append(hole.said)
        if wrong == "" {
          wrong = hole.wrong
        }
        at = close + closer().byteLength()
      }
      copied = at
    } else {
      at = at + 1
    }
  }
  out.appendSlice(template, copied, end)
  Html(said: out.finish(), wrong: wrong)
}

/// What a hole put into the page, and what went wrong putting it there.
///
/// Two fields because a hole has three outcomes and not two: some text,
/// nothing at all, and a problem. Both empty is the middle one, and since an
/// index past the end became an error there is exactly one way to reach it —
/// a list with no elements, put in whole.
struct Hole {
  said: String
  wrong: String
}

/// A hole that found nothing to put in and nothing to complain about.
///
/// An empty list of markup, and nothing else. A list a page loops over is a
/// list of rows and no rows is no markup, which is a page doing its job —
/// unlike `<%= rows[0] %>` against the same list, where the template claimed
/// there was a first row.
fn nothing() -> Hole {
  Hole(said: "", wrong: "")
}

/// A hole whose path asked for something `data` cannot have.
///
/// The message names the path, which is what has to be fixed, and the
/// template it was in, which is where to fix it. The template goes in whole
/// rather than quoted around the hole: this is read once, by whoever is
/// looking at the failing test, and the markup around a hole is how they will
/// recognise which of a page's templates it is.
fn noSuchPath(path: String, template: String) -> Hole {
  Hole(said: "", wrong: "no such path `{path}` in template \"{template}\"")
}

/// What opens a hole.
///
/// Written once rather than as three byte comparisons, because [`fill`] needs
/// its length to step over it and [`unclosed`] needs its text to name it, and
/// a delimiter spelled in three places is a delimiter that will one day be
/// spelled two ways.
fn opener() -> String {
  "<%="
}

/// What closes a hole.
fn closer() -> String {
  "%>"
}

/// Whether the byte at `at` begins a hole: the three bytes of [`opener`].
///
/// The `=` is part of it. `<%` on its own is ERB's statement form, which this
/// module has no use for — the `for` and the `if` are the language's — so a
/// `<%` that is not a `<%=` is text like anything else.
///
/// Every byte of the delimiter is ASCII, and no character of any other value
/// begins with one, so this walks `template` a byte at a time without decoding
/// it and can never stop inside a character.
fn opensHole(template: String, at: Int, end: Int) -> Bool {
  if template.byteAt(at) != '<' {
    return false
  }
  if at + 2 >= end {
    return false
  }
  if template.byteAt(at + 1) != '%' {
    return false
  }
  template.byteAt(at + 2) == '='
}

/// Where the run of name bytes starting at `at` ends, which is `at` itself
/// when there is none.
fn nameEnd(template: String, at: Int, end: Int) -> Int {
  var stop = at
  while stop < end {
    if !isNameByte(template.byteAt(stop)) {
      return stop
    }
    stop = stop + 1
  }
  stop
}

/// Where the run of digits starting at `at` ends, which is `at` itself when
/// there is none.
fn digitEnd(template: String, at: Int, end: Int) -> Int {
  var stop = at
  while stop < end {
    if !isDigitByte(template.byteAt(stop)) {
      return stop
    }
    stop = stop + 1
  }
  stop
}

/// Whether `byte` may appear in a path segment's name: a letter, a digit or
/// `_`.
fn isNameByte(byte: Int) -> Bool {
  if isDigitByte(byte) {
    return true
  }
  if byte == '_' {
    return true
  }
  if byte >= 'a' {
    return byte <= 'z'
  }
  if byte >= 'A' {
    return byte <= 'Z'
  }
  false
}

/// Whether `byte` is one of `0` through `9`.
fn isDigitByte(byte: Int) -> Bool {
  if byte < '0' {
    return false
  }
  byte <= '9'
}

/// What the hole whose path is `path` puts in, escaped or not as the type the
/// path reaches decides, and the problem it ran into if it ran into one.
///
/// `path` is the text between the delimiters with its space trimmed off, so
/// this reads a path and nothing else: `.`, or a name, or a name with `[n]`
/// after it, joined by `.`. **The whole of it has to be a path.** A trailing
/// `.`, a bracket with no number in it, a space in the middle — each is a
/// mistake in the template and answers [`noSuchPath`], where a scanner that
/// had no closing delimiter to stop at could only read as far as it
/// understood and leave the rest as markup. So is an index past the end: the
/// walk stops at the index rather than carrying on into a child that is not
/// there, so `<%= rows[9].at %>` against four rows is refused for `rows[9]`
/// and never asks about `at`.
///
/// `template` is carried only so a message can name it. It is read on the
/// failing path and nowhere else.
fn place(root: DynamicView, path: String, template: String) -> Hole {
  let end = path.byteLength()
  if end == 0 {
    return noSuchPath(path, template)
  }
  if end == 1 && path.byteAt(0) == '.' {
    return placed(root)
  }
  var view = root
  var at = 0
  while at < end {
    if at > 0 {
      if path.byteAt(at) != '.' {
        return noSuchPath(path, template)
      }
      at = at + 1
    }
    let stop = nameEnd(path, at, end)
    if stop == at {
      return noSuchPath(path, template)
    }
    let field = fieldOf(view, core.stringSlice(path, at, stop - at))
    if field < 0 {
      return noSuchPath(path, template)
    }
    view = core.dynamicChild(view, field)
    at = stop
    while at < end {
      if path.byteAt(at) != '[' {
        break
      }
      let digits = digitEnd(path, at + 1, end)
      if digits == at + 1 || digits >= end || path.byteAt(digits) != ']' {
        return noSuchPath(path, template)
      }
      if !isList(core.dynamicKind(view)) {
        return noSuchPath(path, template)
      }
      let index = number(path, at + 1, digits)
      if index >= core.dynamicChildCount(view) {
        return noSuchPath(path, template)
      }
      view = core.dynamicChild(view, index)
      at = digits + 1
    }
  }
  placed(view)
}

/// What a view puts in: markup whole, a list of markup one after another, and
/// anything else rendered and escaped.
///
/// **This is where a problem propagates.** An `Html` carries one, so a
/// fragment built from a template with a bad hole in it hands that problem to
/// whatever puts the fragment in, and a list of markup hands on the first
/// problem any of its parts carries. The page the outermost `fill` answers is
/// therefore refused by `render` wherever in the composition the mistake was
/// written, which is the only arrangement worth having: a page is assembled
/// out of a dozen small templates and the broken one is rarely the outer.
fn placed(view: DynamicView) -> Hole {
  if isHtml(view) {
    return Hole(
      said: core.dynamicString(core.dynamicChild(view, 0)),
      wrong: core.dynamicString(core.dynamicChild(view, 1)),
    )
  }
  if isList(core.dynamicKind(view)) {
    let count = core.dynamicChildCount(view)
    if count == 0 {
      return nothing()
    }
    if everyChildIsHtml(view, count) {
      var out = StringBuilder.withCapacity(16 * count)
      var wrong = ""
      var at = 0
      while at < count {
        let part = core.dynamicChild(view, at)
        out.append(core.dynamicString(core.dynamicChild(part, 0)))
        if wrong == "" {
          wrong = core.dynamicString(core.dynamicChild(part, 1))
        }
        at = at + 1
      }
      return Hole(said: out.finish(), wrong: wrong)
    }
  }
  Hole(said: escape(renderValue(view, maxDepth())), wrong: "")
}

/// Whether `kind` is an `Array` or a `Vector`, the two that
/// `<%= items[2] %>` indexes and the two that can hold a list of markup.
fn isList(kind: Int) -> Bool {
  // Array, Vector
  kind == 8 || kind == 9
}

/// Whether every one of `view`'s `count` children is an [`Html`].
///
/// Every child and not only the first: a list whose type is `Array<Html>` is
/// settled by its first element, but a list of `Any` is not, and a list that
/// is markup in part is not markup.
fn everyChildIsHtml(view: DynamicView, count: Int) -> Bool {
  var at = 0
  while at < count {
    if !isHtml(core.dynamicChild(view, at)) {
      return false
    }
    at = at + 1
  }
  true
}

/// What [`render`] says about a `<%=` that is never closed.
///
/// Nothing escapes any of this. A message goes to whoever is reading a
/// failing test, not onto a page, so the one rule about where a marker could
/// land and what it could close is gone with the marker — which is a rule
/// fewer to get wrong.
fn unclosed(template: String) -> String {
  "an unclosed `{opener()}` in template \"{template}\""
}

/// The number the digits from `at` up to `to` spell, stopping at
/// [`tooFarAlong`].
///
/// A template is text somebody typed, so the digits in it are not bounded by
/// anything, and `<%= words[99999999999999999999] %>` must be refused as a
/// path that misses rather than stop the run on an overflow. Nothing is lost
/// by stopping: an index at the bound is past the end of every collection a
/// run can hold, so it reaches nothing either way.
fn number(template: String, at: Int, to: Int) -> Int {
  var value = 0
  var scan = at
  while scan < to {
    if value >= tooFarAlong() {
      return tooFarAlong()
    }
    value = value * 10 + (template.byteAt(scan) - '0')
    scan = scan + 1
  }
  value
}

/// An index no collection has: where [`number`] stops counting.
fn tooFarAlong() -> Int {
  1000000000000000
}

/// Which child of `view` is the field called `name`, or `-1`.
///
/// Only a struct has fields, and an `opaque` struct publishes none: a path
/// stops at one rather than reading what its module did not export, which is
/// what keeps `<%= who.said %>` from reaching inside an [`Html`].
fn fieldOf(view: DynamicView, name: String) -> Int {
  // Struct
  if core.dynamicKind(view) != 6 {
    return -1
  }
  if core.dynamicOpaque(view) {
    return -1
  }
  let count = core.dynamicChildCount(view)
  var at = 0
  while at < count {
    if core.dynamicFieldName(view, at) == name {
      return at
    }
    at = at + 1
  }
  -1
}

/// Whether `view` names an [`Html`]: the one type whose text goes in whole.
///
/// There is no type identity to ask for — `core.dynamicTypeName` answers a
/// declared name with no module on it — so this asks for the whole shape this
/// module's declaration has: an `opaque` struct called `Html` with two
/// `String` fields, the markup and the problem. A program that declares its
/// own `export opaque struct Html { said: String, wrong: String }` would be
/// taken for this one; nothing can tell them apart from here, and the cost of
/// being wrong is that a program's own type is trusted as markup, which is a
/// thing that program could have done with [`markup`] anyway.
fn isHtml(view: DynamicView) -> Bool {
  // Struct
  if core.dynamicKind(view) != 6 {
    return false
  }
  if !core.dynamicOpaque(view) {
    return false
  }
  if core.dynamicTypeName(view) != "Html" {
    return false
  }
  if core.dynamicChildCount(view) != 2 {
    return false
  }
  // String
  if core.dynamicKind(core.dynamicChild(view, 0)) != 5 {
    return false
  }
  // String
  core.dynamicKind(core.dynamicChild(view, 1)) == 5
}

/// `text` with the five characters that mean something to a parser written as
/// entities.
///
/// `&` first, because the others are written with one. `"` and `'` are here
/// and not only `<` and `>`, because a hole is as likely to be an attribute's
/// value as it is to be between tags, and `<a title="<%= said %>">` has to
/// hold a quote without ending the attribute.
///
/// Nothing is copied until the first character that needs one: text with none
/// of the five — which most text is — is appended in one piece at the end.
fn escape(text: String) -> String {
  let end = text.byteLength()
  var out = StringBuilder.withCapacity(end)
  var at = 0
  var copied = 0
  while at < end {
    let named = entity(text.byteAt(at))
    if named != "" {
      out.appendSlice(text, copied, at)
      out.append(named)
      copied = at + 1
    }
    at = at + 1
  }
  out.appendSlice(text, copied, end)
  out.finish()
}

/// The entity `byte` is written as, or `""` where it is written as itself.
fn entity(byte: Int) -> String {
  if byte == '&' {
    return "&amp;"
  }
  if byte == '<' {
    return "&lt;"
  }
  if byte == '>' {
    return "&gt;"
  }
  if byte == '"' {
    return "&quot;"
  }
  if byte == 39 {
    return "&#39;"
  }
  ""
}

/// How far [`renderValue`] descends before it writes `…`.
///
/// A value that holds itself through a `Vector` has no end to render, and the
/// walk `std.dynamic` uses to say so needs the `RenderPath` the lowering hands
/// it, which nothing in Cove can make. So this walk is bounded instead. Page
/// data is a view model — a struct of scalars, or a list of them — and sixteen
/// levels is far past anything a hole is given on purpose.
fn maxDepth() -> Int {
  16
}

/// The text the language writes for what `view` names: what `"{value}"` gives,
/// kind for kind, down to [`maxDepth`] levels.
///
/// This is `std.dynamic`'s rendering written again rather than called, and
/// that is not a choice. `std.dynamic.renderInto` takes a `RenderPath` — the
/// path of vectors the walk that reached a box was inside — and a `RenderPath`
/// is made by the lowering and by nothing else: `core` has no intrinsic that
/// answers one, so a standard-library module that is not the lowering's callee
/// cannot produce one to pass. What it costs is this function; what it buys is
/// that a hole holding an `Int` needs no stringifying at the call site, which
/// is the whole of the third row of [`fill`]'s table.
fn renderValue(view: DynamicView, depth: Int) -> String {
  let kind = core.dynamicKind(view)
  // String
  if kind == 5 {
    return core.dynamicString(view)
  }
  // Int
  if kind == 2 {
    return "{core.dynamicInt(view)}"
  }
  // Bool
  if kind == 1 {
    return "{core.dynamicBool(view)}"
  }
  // Float
  if kind == 3 {
    return "{core.dynamicFloat(view)}"
  }
  // Duration
  if kind == 4 {
    return "{core.dynamicDuration(view)}"
  }
  // Unit
  if kind == 0 {
    return "()"
  }
  // Range
  if kind == 12 {
    let from = core.dynamicInt(core.dynamicChild(view, 0))
    let to = core.dynamicInt(core.dynamicChild(view, 1))
    if core.dynamicBool(core.dynamicChild(view, 2)) {
      return "{from}..{to}"
    }
    return "{from}..<{to}"
  }
  // Function
  if kind == 13 {
    return "<fn>"
  }
  // Opaque
  if kind == 14 {
    return core.dynamicHandleText(view)
  }
  // Shared
  if kind == 15 {
    return "<shared>"
  }
  if depth <= 0 {
    return "…"
  }
  // Struct
  if kind == 6 {
    return renderRecord(view, depth)
  }
  // Enum
  if kind == 7 {
    if core.dynamicChildCount(view) == 0 {
      return core.dynamicCaseName(view)
    }
    return renderParts(view, core.dynamicCaseName(view), "(", ")", depth)
  }
  // Array
  if kind == 8 {
    return renderParts(view, "", "[", "]", depth)
  }
  // Vector
  if kind == 9 {
    return renderParts(view, "", "[", "]", depth)
  }
  // Set
  if kind == 10 {
    return renderParts(view, "", "\{", "\}", depth)
  }
  // Map
  if kind == 11 {
    return renderPairs(view, depth)
  }
  unnamedKind()
  ""
}

/// [`renderValue`] of a struct: `Name(field: value, …)`, an `opaque` one as
/// its name alone, and the builtin `Error` as its message — the three forms
/// `Display for Value` has always written.
fn renderRecord(view: DynamicView, depth: Int) -> String {
  if core.dynamicOpaque(view) {
    return core.dynamicTypeName(view)
  }
  let count = core.dynamicChildCount(view)
  if count == 1 {
    if core.dynamicTypeName(view) == "Error" {
      if core.dynamicFieldName(view, 0) == "message" {
        return renderValue(core.dynamicChild(view, 0), depth - 1)
      }
    }
  }
  var out = StringBuilder.withCapacity(16)
  out.append(core.dynamicTypeName(view))
  out.append("(")
  var at = 0
  while at < count {
    if at > 0 {
      out.append(", ")
    }
    out.append(core.dynamicFieldName(view, at))
    out.append(": ")
    out.append(renderValue(core.dynamicChild(view, at), depth - 1))
    at = at + 1
  }
  out.append(")")
  out.finish()
}

/// [`renderValue`] of a container whose children are written one after
/// another: `head`, then `open`, the children separated by `, `, and `close`.
fn renderParts(
  view: DynamicView,
  head: String,
  open: String,
  close: String,
  depth: Int,
) -> String {
  let count = core.dynamicChildCount(view)
  var out = StringBuilder.withCapacity(16)
  out.append(head)
  out.append(open)
  var at = 0
  while at < count {
    if at > 0 {
      out.append(", ")
    }
    out.append(renderValue(core.dynamicChild(view, at), depth - 1))
    at = at + 1
  }
  out.append(close)
  out.finish()
}

/// [`renderValue`] of a `Map`: `{key: value, …}`, whose children alternate
/// key, value, key, value in the one ascending order a map has.
fn renderPairs(view: DynamicView, depth: Int) -> String {
  let count = core.dynamicChildCount(view)
  var out = StringBuilder.withCapacity(16)
  out.append("\{")
  var at = 0
  while at < count {
    if at > 0 {
      if at % 2 == 0 {
        out.append(", ")
      } else {
        out.append(": ")
      }
    }
    out.append(renderValue(core.dynamicChild(view, at), depth - 1))
    at = at + 1
  }
  out.append("\}")
  out.finish()
}

/// Stops the run: [`renderValue`] was handed a kind it does not name.
///
/// `std.dynamic.unclassified`'s reason, in this module's words. A kind is a
/// name and never a range, so a code appended to `cove_ir::DynamicKind` does
/// not quietly join whichever class its number falls beside — it comes here
/// until somebody decides what a page does with it.
fn unnamedKind() {
  let none = ""
  core.refuse(
    "`std.html` was handed a value of a kind none of its walks names: this is a fault in Cove's standard library, not in the program",
    none,
    none,
  )
}
